<!--Post: 4752--><!--Time: 1106712353-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4752"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rebtk<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rebtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 8:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tom, <BR>Great article.  I&#39;ve done a little reserch into the letter that you posted. One thing about BTK is that he is money conscience &#40;tight with his money&#41; I say that because he refers to the taxpayer.  He could also be a political activist.  Some of the jobs that he has held would be of a military background particularly the Navy. He also could have been a Rancher, shipyard worker, long shoreman, &amp; or a trucker.  My reasoning is because of the knots he mentions.  They would be specific to these kinds of jobs. A Rancher would hogtie something with a slip knot to hold the leg down and tie hand behind the back. That was mentioned in the letter. I also did a diagram with a plus sign and put North, South, East, &amp; West and drew where the bodies were placed and I come up with a peace sign, which can signify a political activist.  Some one from the 60&#39;s that protested the Vietnam war.  But this is what I came up with I hope that it helps.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
